1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,033
This is a planet with no star.

2
00:00:02,353 --> 00:00:06,022
Five times heavier than Jupiter, drifting
alone through the dark.

3
00:00:06,342 --> 00:00:10,451
And it's heading into our Solar System, on
a path that crosses Earth's orbit.

4
00:00:11,251 --> 00:00:14,489
We simulated its pass seven hundred and
thirty times,

5
00:00:14,489 --> 00:00:17,177
changing just one thing: the day it
arrives.

6
00:00:17,497 --> 00:00:19,854
On most dates, Earth barely notices.

7
00:00:20,174 --> 00:00:23,559
On others, Earth is thrown out of the
Solar System, forever.

8
00:00:23,879 --> 00:00:27,468
And on a handful of dates, something much
stranger happens to the Moon.

9
00:00:28,468 --> 00:00:29,859
Rogue planets are real.

10
00:00:30,179 --> 00:00:33,911
Planets are born around stars. But in a
young solar system,

11
00:00:33,911 --> 00:00:37,959
giant planets can fling each other out,
into interstellar space.

12
00:00:38,279 --> 00:00:41,656
We find them when one drifts in front of a
distant star.

13
00:00:41,656 --> 00:00:45,997
Its gravity bends that star's light, and
the star briefly gets brighter.

14
00:00:46,317 --> 00:00:49,438
One study in twenty twenty-three suggested
our galaxy

15
00:00:49,438 --> 00:00:52,853
could hold more of these lonely planets
than it has stars.

16
00:00:53,453 --> 00:00:58,365
Ours, in this story, is five times the
mass of Jupiter, and about the same size.

17
00:00:58,685 --> 00:01:01,613
Without a sun, it's dark. It glows only
faintly,

18
00:01:01,613 --> 00:01:04,297
with the heat left over from when it
formed.

19
00:01:04,617 --> 00:01:08,108
There's a real object like this just seven
light-years away.

20
00:01:08,108 --> 00:01:10,320
It's called WISE zero eight five five.

21
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:13,435
Its clouds are about as cold as a kitchen
freezer.

22
00:01:13,755 --> 00:01:16,499
And NASA's Roman Space Telescope is
designed

23
00:01:16,499 --> 00:01:19,243
to find hundreds more rogue planets like
it.

24
00:01:19,843 --> 00:01:22,521
We'd see ours coming, long before it got
here.

25
00:01:22,841 --> 00:01:26,252
Once it's about a thousand times farther
away than the Sun,

26
00:01:26,252 --> 00:01:30,011
sunlight glinting off it is enough for
today's survey telescopes.

27
00:01:30,331 --> 00:01:34,590
At twenty kilometres per second, that's
more than two hundred years of warning.

28
00:01:34,910 --> 00:01:37,805
Within weeks, astronomers would know its
path.

29
00:01:37,805 --> 00:01:42,148
It passes the Sun at exactly Earth's
distance, and crosses our orbit.

30
00:01:42,748 --> 00:01:43,856
What would it look like?

31
00:01:44,176 --> 00:01:48,308
About seven and a half years before it
arrives, it becomes visible to

32
00:01:48,308 --> 00:01:52,441
the naked eye. A faint new star, creeping
through the constellations.

33
00:01:52,761 --> 00:01:54,957
Eight months out, it's brighter than
Jupiter.

34
00:01:55,277 --> 00:01:58,247
Five months out, it outshines Venus. After
the Moon,

35
00:01:58,247 --> 00:02:00,646
it's the brightest thing in the night sky.

36
00:02:00,966 --> 00:02:05,198
In binoculars, it isn't a point any more.
It's a tiny, banded disc.

37
00:02:05,518 --> 00:02:10,091
And on the worst dates, in the final two
weeks, it grows wider than the full Moon.

38
00:02:10,691 --> 00:02:11,914
And here's the problem.

39
00:02:12,234 --> 00:02:16,064
We can't push it aside. It's sixteen
hundred times heavier than Earth.

40
00:02:16,383 --> 00:02:18,257
We can't move Earth out of the way,
either.

41
00:02:18,577 --> 00:02:21,005
So everything comes down to one question.

42
00:02:21,005 --> 00:02:23,550
Where will Earth be, on the day it
arrives?

43
00:02:24,150 --> 00:02:28,868
Earth moves about two and a half million
kilometres along its orbit every day.

44
00:02:29,188 --> 00:02:33,709
So we ran the pass for every possible
arrival date, in steps of half a day.

45
00:02:34,029 --> 00:02:36,975
Seven hundred and thirty runs. The Sun,
the rogue,

46
00:02:36,975 --> 00:02:40,570
all eight planets, and the Moon, tracked
all the way through.

47
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:42,657
Here's what the calendar looks like.

48
00:02:43,257 --> 00:02:47,039
On about three dates out of four, Earth
stays almost where it is.

49
00:02:47,039 --> 00:02:49,542
The year gets a few days longer or
shorter.

50
00:02:49,862 --> 00:02:53,234
On about one in six, the year changes by
weeks, or months.

51
00:02:53,554 --> 00:02:57,204
But on roughly one date in ten, the world
as we know it ends.

52
00:02:57,524 --> 00:03:02,252
There are three ways it can go. And which
one we get is decided by a matter of days.

53
00:03:02,852 --> 00:03:05,992
If it arrives about two weeks after Earth
has gone by,

54
00:03:05,992 --> 00:03:07,794
it crosses our orbit behind us.

55
00:03:08,114 --> 00:03:11,418
Its gravity tugs Earth backwards. Earth
slows down.

56
00:03:11,738 --> 00:03:14,767
And a planet that slows down falls closer
to the Sun.

57
00:03:15,367 --> 00:03:17,761
Earth settles onto a new, tighter orbit.

58
00:03:18,081 --> 00:03:20,724
A year now lasts two hundred and
eighty-two days.

59
00:03:21,044 --> 00:03:24,474
At its closest, Earth swings in almost as
far as Venus,

60
00:03:24,474 --> 00:03:27,094
and gets twice the sunlight it gets today.

61
00:03:27,414 --> 00:03:31,060
From the ground, the Sun looks forty
percent wider than it does now.

62
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:35,683
Averaged over the year, that's more than
forty percent extra sunlight.

63
00:03:36,003 --> 00:03:38,841
Climate models suggest that around ten
percent

64
00:03:38,841 --> 00:03:42,050
extra would be enough to start a runaway
greenhouse.

65
00:03:42,370 --> 00:03:44,936
The oceans would slowly boil off into
space,

66
00:03:44,936 --> 00:03:47,736
the way scientists think they once did on
Venus.

67
00:03:48,336 --> 00:03:52,532
But move the arrival date by just four
days, and something stranger happens.

68
00:03:52,852 --> 00:03:55,654
The rogue passes ten million kilometres
from us.

69
00:03:55,654 --> 00:03:58,340
Twenty-seven times farther away than the
Moon.

70
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:01,089
That's not close enough to rip the Moon
away.

71
00:04:01,089 --> 00:04:03,086
But it shakes the Moon's orbit loose.

72
00:04:03,686 --> 00:04:07,303
Earth falls onto an even tighter orbit. At
its closest,

73
00:04:07,303 --> 00:04:10,065
it swings in nearer to the Sun than Venus.

74
00:04:10,385 --> 00:04:12,471
For four months, nothing seems wrong.

75
00:04:12,791 --> 00:04:15,872
Then, a hundred and twenty-three days
after the pass,

76
00:04:15,872 --> 00:04:18,720
the Sun's gravity pulls the Moon away from
Earth.

77
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,210
The Moon becomes a planet.

78
00:04:21,530 --> 00:04:25,401
It goes around the Sun on its own now, on
an orbit close to ours.

79
00:04:25,721 --> 00:04:30,359
No more months. No more eclipses of the
Moon. Nights without a Moon, for good.

80
00:04:30,679 --> 00:04:33,845
The tides wouldn't stop. The Sun raises
tides too,

81
00:04:33,845 --> 00:04:38,087
and on this tighter orbit, at times, even
bigger ones than today's.

82
00:04:38,687 --> 00:04:40,447
The strangest part is who did it.

83
00:04:40,767 --> 00:04:43,800
It wasn't the rogue that stole the Moon.
It was the Sun.

84
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:47,607
The rogue only loosened it, from ten
million kilometres away.

85
00:04:47,927 --> 00:04:51,632
And it's a coin toss. On these dates,
whether we keep the Moon

86
00:04:51,632 --> 00:04:55,696
depends on where it happens to be in its
orbit as the rogue goes by.

87
00:04:56,296 --> 00:04:59,289
Now let it arrive a week or two before
Earth gets there,

88
00:04:59,289 --> 00:05:01,373
so it crosses our orbit in front of us.

89
00:05:01,693 --> 00:05:05,779
Its gravity pulls Earth forwards. Earth
speeds up, and swings outward.

90
00:05:06,379 --> 00:05:10,935
The new orbit is long and stretched. One
year now lasts two years and eight months.

91
00:05:11,255 --> 00:05:14,986
At its farthest, Earth is out past Mars,
in the asteroid belt,

92
00:05:14,986 --> 00:05:18,055
almost three times farther from the Sun
than today.

93
00:05:18,375 --> 00:05:21,568
Out there, the Sun looks a third as wide
as it does today,

94
00:05:21,568 --> 00:05:23,605
and its light is an eighth as strong.

95
00:05:24,205 --> 00:05:28,443
Averaged over that long year, Earth gets
less sunlight than Mars does today.

96
00:05:28,763 --> 00:05:33,182
For more than two years of every orbit, it
gets less than half of today's sunlight.

97
00:05:33,502 --> 00:05:38,229
The oceans freeze over from the top down.
And even at its closest to the Sun,

98
00:05:38,229 --> 00:05:40,930
Earth never warms up as much as it does
now.

99
00:05:41,250 --> 00:05:44,942
Every two years and eight months, it
swings back in for about six

100
00:05:44,942 --> 00:05:48,521
months of weak sunlight. Then it heads out
into the cold again.

101
00:05:49,121 --> 00:05:51,126
And then there's the window in the middle.

102
00:05:51,446 --> 00:05:55,917
Eleven and a half days in the calendar. If
the rogue arrives inside them,

103
00:05:55,917 --> 00:05:59,225
it passes so close to Earth that there's
no saving us.

104
00:05:59,825 --> 00:06:04,386
On the worst day, it misses us by two
hundred and eleven thousand kilometres.

105
00:06:04,386 --> 00:06:05,630
Closer than the Moon.

106
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:09,210
In our sky, it would be almost forty
degrees wide.

107
00:06:09,210 --> 00:06:11,687
Seventy-five full Moons, side by side.

108
00:06:12,007 --> 00:06:14,360
Its tidal pull would be nearly eight
hundred

109
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:16,499
thousand times stronger than the Moon's.

110
00:06:17,099 --> 00:06:20,045
That's just thirty thousand kilometres
outside the

111
00:06:20,045 --> 00:06:23,462
distance where its tides would tear a
liquid planet apart.

112
00:06:23,782 --> 00:06:28,007
For a few hours, the oceans, the rock and
the air are all pulled towards it.

113
00:06:28,327 --> 00:06:30,877
Then it slingshots Earth out of the Solar
System.

114
00:06:31,197 --> 00:06:35,998
The whole encounter, from first tug to
final fling, is over in about two weeks.

115
00:06:36,598 --> 00:06:39,254
Earth leaves at forty kilometres per
second,

116
00:06:39,254 --> 00:06:41,969
faster than it has ever moved around the
Sun.

117
00:06:42,289 --> 00:06:44,588
The Moon is flung away on a path of its
own.

118
00:06:44,908 --> 00:06:48,380
Two years later, Earth is almost as far
out as Uranus,

119
00:06:48,380 --> 00:06:50,694
and the Sun is just a dazzling star.

120
00:06:51,294 --> 00:06:54,337
The oceans freeze over. Only deep below
the ice,

121
00:06:54,337 --> 00:06:58,900
around volcanic vents on the sea floor,
could life carry on in the dark.

122
00:06:59,220 --> 00:07:03,126
On a few dates at the very edge of the
window, we'd at least keep the Moon.

123
00:07:03,446 --> 00:07:06,922
Earth and Moon would leave together. Two
travellers, with no sun.

124
00:07:07,242 --> 00:07:09,574
Days and nights would still come and go,

125
00:07:09,574 --> 00:07:12,664
as the Earth turned. The sky would simply
stay black.

126
00:07:13,864 --> 00:07:14,904
So, should you worry?

127
00:07:15,224 --> 00:07:19,076
For a planet like this to pass this close
to the Sun, you'd expect to

128
00:07:19,076 --> 00:07:23,152
wait hundreds of thousands of times longer
than the universe has existed.

129
00:07:23,472 --> 00:07:27,641
In four and a half billion years, it has
almost certainly never happened to us.

130
00:07:28,241 --> 00:07:30,631
But it's a strange thing to learn about
our planet.

131
00:07:30,951 --> 00:07:34,405
Everything we know depends on where Earth
is, on a single day.

132
00:07:34,725 --> 00:07:38,331
Astronomers would know that date more than
two hundred years ahead.

133
00:07:38,331 --> 00:07:40,377
And they'd know exactly what it meant.

134
00:07:40,697 --> 00:07:44,321
Eleven and a half days on the calendar are
all that separate

135
00:07:44,321 --> 00:07:48,065
an ordinary year from a journey into the
dark that never ends.

136
00:07:49,065 --> 00:07:51,024
Next time, something does hit us.

137
00:07:51,524 --> 00:07:56,006
Sixty-six million years ago, an asteroid
wider than a city struck the Earth.

138
00:07:56,006 --> 00:07:59,191
We'll go through what happened next,
minute by minute.

139
00:08:00,791 --> 00:08:03,261
Subscribe, and we'll turn the next dial
together.

